# The Goatherd's Count (side quest, docs/story/side_quests.md): Goodwife Petra's grandson Erno keeps the village's goats
# on the high pasture above the road, and every evening for a week he has counted one more goat than he owns
# (data/locations/barovia_high_pasture.json). The extra one is an old nanny goat with a bell, and the dog won't go
# near her. **Twist:** she's Snowdrop, the nanny Petra's father Ilarion was leading the night he walked into the mists
# for a doctor, fifty years ago (The Last Traveller): the mists are giving back what they took. **Escalation:** the
# mists don't give anything back for nothing, and the wolves come out of them after her. Reached from Goodwife Petra at
# the well (village_of_barovia/townsfolk:goodwife_menu) and Erno on the pasture by evening; Speak with Animals lets
# Snowdrop and the flock tell it themselves. Every line here is unvoiced for now.
# Reshaped (NPC plan batch 1): the wolves aren't certain. Once the bell is read, the evening becomes a race against the
# dusk: three beats (stragglers, a gap in the wall, the cracked bell), each a roll or a shrug, and a goat lost to the
# fog on every miss. Two lost and the mist sends its wolves; fewer and the flock is in, the white finds nothing to push
# at and the fight is won without a blow (xp fight). Lines drafted by Gemini, edited.

# --- Goodwife Petra at the well (village_of_barovia/townsfolk:goodwife_menu jumps here) ---

~ petra
Goodwife Petra: The goats? My grandson Erno's, or the village's; he minds them all for a copper a head. Up on the high pasture above the road.
Goodwife Petra: He came down last night white as milk. Says he counts one more every evening than he has. Twenty-three goats, and he counts twenty-four, and the dog won't go near the extra one. He's a good boy. He can count.
quest the_goatherds_count asked
-> END

# --- Erno at the fold, evenings (npc pasture_erno) ---

~ erno
if quest.the_goatherds_count == done
if flag.snowdrop_home
Erno: Twenty-three. (He beams.) And Snowdrop sleeps in Gran's kitchen, so she doesn't count. Gran says that's the most beautiful number there is.
elif flag.snowdrop_back
Erno: Twenty-three. Every night. (He looks up at the fog, and then doesn't.) I can still hear the bell, sometimes. Gran says that's all right.
endif
-> END
endif
if flag.pasture_wolves_beaten or flag.pasture_flock_safe
-> after
endif
if flag.pasture_erno_met
Erno: (Counting under his breath.) Twenty-two, twenty-three. Twenty-four. (He stops.) There. Her again. By the wall.
-> erno_menu
endif
set pasture_erno_met
Narrator: A boy of ten or so in a coat too big for him stands at the gate of a dry-stone fold, touching each goat on the back as it goes past him, with a big grey dog pressed against his legs.
Erno: Twenty-one, twenty-two, twenty-three. (A long pause.) Twenty-four.
Narrator: The last one through the gate is an old nanny goat, white going yellow, with a cracked bell on a leather collar. The dog whines and won't look at her.
-> erno_menu

~ erno_menu
* [Animal Handling DC 10] (Go and look at the old nanny goat.) -> nanny | nanny_shy
* (Look at the bell on her collar.) -> bell
* [if spell:speak_with_animals] Ask the old nanny goat where she's been. -> snowdrop_speaks
* [if not spell:speak_with_animals] [knows:speak_with_animals] (Cast Speak with Animals, and ask the old nanny goat where she's been.) -> snowdrop_cast
* (Leave him to his counting.) -> END

~ nanny
Narrator: She lets you close. Her coat is damp, and cold, the way mist is cold, though the evening's dry. Burrs in her coat from bushes that don't grow on this side of the valley. Her eyes are very old and very calm.
-> bell

~ nanny_shy
Narrator: She walks off along the wall, unhurried, and the bell on her collar clanks once. It's a sound the whole pasture seems to stop and listen to.
-> bell

~ bell
Narrator: The bell is cracked and green, and scratched into the collar's leather, in letters fifty years old: SNOWDROP. And a little mark like a boot.
if flag.ilarion_met
Narrator: You've seen that mark before: on a pair of good grey boots, on a young man by the edge of the fog who asked you what day it was.
endif
Erno: Gran had a nanny called Snowdrop, when she was little. Great-grandad took her with him the night he went into the fog. Gran says she still hears the bell sometimes, at the edge of the mists. (He swallows.) Gran says a lot of things.
# Once the wolves are beaten (or never came), the bell leads back to choosing where she goes, not to another fight (Wiki finding 9).
if flag.pasture_wolves_beaten or flag.pasture_flock_safe
-> after
endif
-> flock_start

~ snowdrop_cast
cast speak_with_animals
if check.last
-> snowdrop_speaks
endif
-> erno_menu

# Speak with Animals (2024 PHB): fifty years in the mists, waiting for the man in the grey boots.
~ snowdrop_speaks
Narrator: She takes her time answering, the way very old goats take their time over everything. Her voice, when it comes, is cool and calm, like mist on a window.
Narrator: "I walked into the white with the man in the grey boots. He said: wait here, Snowdrop, I'll be back with the doctor. So I waited. The white is long."
if not flag.ilarion_home
Narrator: "He's still in it. On a stone by the road, where the white starts, asking what day it is. He never was any good at waiting."
endif
Narrator: "The white let go of me. It doesn't let go for nothing. It wants something back, and it's sending for it." She looks up the pasture, at the fog.
-> bell

# --- Getting the flock in before dark: each miss costs a goat to the fog ---

~ flock_start
set goat_strays = 0
Narrator: The last of the light goes a thin, cold orange along the ridge. Up on the slope, the goats stop grazing and begin to drift toward the white wall of the mist.
Narrator: The big grey dog sits down hard against your boots and will not look up the hill.
Erno [afraid]: The light's going. Help me get them inside the stones before the dark comes down.
Erno [afraid]: Please. Don't let them walk into the white.
-> round1

~ round1
Narrator: Half a dozen stragglers have wandered up the steep grass toward the fog, their tin bells clinking in the quiet.
Narrator: They are twenty paces from the mist, lifting their chins to smell the cold.
* [Animal Handling DC 10] (Call them down with the old herding calls.) -> r1_ok_animal | r1_fail
* [Athletics DC 10] (Run up the slope and herd them downhill.) -> r1_ok_athletics | r1_fail
* (Wave your arms and shout.) -> r1_noroll

~ r1_ok_animal
Narrator: You cup your hands and give the low, whistling pasture-call the herdsmen use when rain is coming.
Narrator: The lead billy twitches an ear, turns, and brings the rest clattering down the slope like a grey tide.
-> round2

~ r1_ok_athletics
Narrator: You take the turf at a dead run, coming around their flank before they can scatter into the cloud.
Narrator: They bolt downhill ahead of you in a tangle of horns and hooves, and you find yourselves laughing for the breath it takes.
-> round2

~ r1_fail
set goat_strays += 1
Narrator: You lunge for the nearest goat, but your boot slips on the wet grass and the knot of them breaks apart.
Narrator: One speckled nanny bounds over the crest, and the white takes her without a sound.
-> round2

~ r1_noroll
set goat_strays += 1
Narrator: You wave your arms and shout up the hill at them.
Narrator: The stragglers regard you with flat yellow eyes, turn their backs, and step calmly into the fog.
-> round2

~ round2
Narrator: Most of the flock is crowding through the gate now, jostling and bleating against the dry-stone wall.
Narrator: Down where the fold meets the open pasture, the old stones have given way, leaving a gap wide enough for a goat's shoulders.
* [Survival DC 10] (Find where they are getting through.) -> r2_ok_survival | r2_fail
* [Investigation DC 10] (Work out which stones fell, and where they go.) -> r2_ok_investigation | r2_fail
* (Shove a pack into the gap.) -> r2_noroll

~ r2_ok_survival
Narrator: You spot the spill of crushed nettles and flattened grass where the wall has slumped outward.
Narrator: Before the press of the flock can find it, you brace your knees and wedge the heavy cornerstone back into the turf.
-> round3

~ r2_ok_investigation
Narrator: The fallen slate lies in the mud in the exact order it slid from the course.
Narrator: Five stones, longest at the bottom, flat side in: you lock them back into the dry-wall before the first goat reaches the corner.
-> round3

~ r2_fail
set goat_strays += 1
Narrator: You search the dark line of the wall for the breach, but the dusk and the jostling backs hide it until too late.
Narrator: A lean black goat slips sideways through the tumble of stones and vanishes up the hillside.
-> round3

~ r2_noroll
set goat_strays += 1
Narrator: You drop a heavy pack into the breach to block the way.
Narrator: An old billy sniffs the canvas once, steps neatly over the straps, and trots out onto the darkening slope.
-> round3

~ round3
Narrator: The old nanny with the scarred ears stands alone at the open gate, refusing to cross the threshold.
Narrator: Every breath she takes sets her cracked iron bell clinking, a flat, tinny note that carries up the hill.
Erno [afraid]: It's the bell. The white hears the bell, that's how it knows where she is.
* [Sleight of Hand DC 10] (Stuff the bell with Erno's scarf.) -> r3_ok_hands | r3_fail
* [Animal Handling DC 12] (Talk the old nanny into the fold.) -> r3_ok_calm | r3_fail
* (Wave her through from a step back.) -> r3_noroll

~ r3_ok_hands
Narrator: Erno unwinds his woollen scarf and you slip two fingers inside the rusted iron rim.
Narrator: One quick twist packs the wool tight around the clapper, and the pasture goes dead quiet.
-> flock_end

~ r3_ok_calm
Narrator: You drop to one knee by the gatepost and speak to her the way you would speak to a tired horse at the end of a long road.
Narrator: She lowers her head, steps over the sill, and takes her place in the corner as if she walked out of it yesterday, not fifty years ago.
-> flock_end

~ r3_fail
set goat_strays += 1
Narrator: You reach for the collar, but she jerks her horns back against the gatepost.
Narrator: The cracked bell strikes the wood once, loud and clear in the hush, and the whole hillside seems to listen.
-> flock_end

~ r3_noroll
set goat_strays += 1
Narrator: You leave the collar alone and try to wave her through from a step back.
Narrator: She stays where she is, chewing nothing, while the cracked bell rings and rings into the dusk.
-> flock_end

~ flock_end
if flag.goat_strays >= 2
-> wolves_come
endif
-> flock_in

# Fewer than two lost: the flock and the old nanny are in, and the white finds nothing to push at.
~ flock_in
xp fight pasture_wolves
set pasture_flock_safe
quest the_goatherds_count defended
Narrator: You drop the ash bar across the gate. The flock is inside the stones, pressed flank to flank in the straw.
Narrator: Up at the top of the pasture, the white wall swells outward like a sail taking wind, hesitates over the empty grass, and settles flat again.
if flag.goat_strays == 0
Erno [smile]: Twenty-one, twenty-two, twenty-three. Twenty-four.
else
Erno: Twenty-one, twenty-two, and Snowdrop makes twenty-three. There were twenty-four, before the white got thick.
Narrator: He rests his chin on the top rail of the fold and looks out at the wall of fog, where the road stops.
Erno [sad]: Gran says the mist gives back what it likes. That was the small brown one with the soft ears, so I hope it likes her.
endif
Erno: The village says she's a wolf-goat, come to lead the others in. She isn't. Her collar says Snowdrop.
Erno [sad]: She just remembered where the fold was. I want her to stay.
-> after

~ wolves_come
Narrator: Up at the top of the pasture, where the grass stops and the fog starts, the white wall of the mists bulges, the way a sheet does when someone pushes on it from the other side.
Narrator: Out of it, low and fast, come wolves. Not the valley's grey wolves; these are white with the mist, and wet, as if they'd come a very long way through it. They're coming for the old nanny.
Erno: The fold! Get them in the fold!
interject tag:kind: Behind the wall, Erno. We've got her.
combat pasture_wolves

# --- After the wolves ---

~ after
Narrator: The goats are all in the fold, crowded together, and the old nanny stands by the gate, quite calm, with mist still beading on her coat. Up at the top of the pasture the fog has gone flat again.
* (Take her down to Goodwife Petra.) -> home
* (Walk her back up to the mists. They'll only send more.) -> back

~ home
set snowdrop_home
quest the_goatherds_count done
Narrator: Goodwife Petra comes up the pasture track with her skirts in her fists, and stops, and puts her hand on the old nanny's head, and the bell clanks once.
Goodwife Petra: Snowdrop. You stupid old thing. Fifty years. (She doesn't cry, quite.)
if flag.ilarion_home
Goodwife Petra: Father's by the fire. He'll cry when he sees you. He cries at everything now; he says he's fifty years behind on it.
else
Goodwife Petra: Where's my father, then? Hm? You left him behind.
endif
Goodwife Petra: (To you.) This was my mother's. Smell it, and the dogs love you, and the goats follow you anywhere. I never had the heart to use it. Use it.
give potion_of_animal_friendship
gold +25
approve liriel_dawnsong +2 kip_smudgewick +2 wren_featherfoot +1: You brought Snowdrop home to Goodwife Petra
-> END

~ back
set snowdrop_back
quest the_goatherds_count done
Narrator: You walk her up to the edge of the fog. She looks back once, at the fold, at the village roofs below, and then walks into the white without hurrying, and the bell goes on for a long time after you can't see her.
Erno: (Very quietly.) Twenty-three.
Narrator: At the gate of the fold, Erno's left something for you: a little stoppered bottle Goodwife Petra gave him for the goats, wrapped in a note that says THANK YOU in big letters.
give potion_of_animal_friendship
gold +25
approve thistle +1 godrick_pendlebrook +1: You gave Snowdrop back to the mists so they'd send nothing after her
-> END

# --- A goat (npc pasture_goat) ---

~ goat
Narrator: A goat. It chews something that isn't grass, and looks at you as if you might be next.
* [if spell:speak_with_animals] Ask the goat what's wrong with the flock. -> goat_speaks
* [if not spell:speak_with_animals] [knows:speak_with_animals] (Cast Speak with Animals, and ask the goat what's wrong with the flock.) -> goat_cast
* [if spell:speak_with_animals or knows:speak_with_animals] Leave it to its chewing. -> END
-> END

~ goat_cast
cast speak_with_animals
if check.last
-> goat_speaks
endif
-> END

~ goat_speaks
if quest.the_goatherds_count == done
Narrator: "Twenty-three of us. The boy counts right now. He sings when he counts right."
-> END
endif
if not quest.the_goatherds_count
quest the_goatherds_count asked
endif
Narrator: "There's one too many of us. The old one with the bell. She came out of the white at the top of the field, and she smells of the other side of it, and the dog won't go near her."
if flag.pasture_wolves_beaten
Narrator: "The wet dogs that came out after her are dead. The white is still looking at her. We can feel it looking."
else
Narrator: "Something's coming after her, in the white. At dusk we can smell it from here. Wet dog. A lot of wet dog."
endif
-> END
